WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Tuples.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:07.733
They look like lists, but one small difference unlocks dictionary keys and multiple return values.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:12.833
A tuple is ordered like a list, but it can't change once it's created.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.573
That isn't a limitation.

5
00:00:14.573 --> 00:00:17.633
It's the feature, because immutable means hashable.

6
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:21.567
And note, it's the comma that makes a tuple, not the parentheses.

7
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:24.600
Here's why hashability matters.

8
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.133
A tuple never changes, so its hash never changes.

9
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:31.200
A list could mutate, so its hash would keep shifting.

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.600
That means a tuple works as a dictionary key, and a list crashes with an unhashable error.

11
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.200
Tuples are also the hidden machinery behind multiple return values.

12
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.233
When a function returns two values with a comma, Python packs them into one tuple.

13
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.967
That single tuple travels back to the caller.

14
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.650
And the caller unpacks it into two names.

15
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.800
Even the classic swap works this way.

16
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.233
Here it is in code, with Halley's comet.

17
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:03.333
The function computes the closest and farthest distance from the sun, and returns both.

18
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.567
We unpack the pair into p and a.

19
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:10.133
Halley swings from point five nine to thirty five astronomical units.

20
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:13.133
Here's a place you'll use this daily.

21
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:16.667
Your agent keeps asking for the same natural event lookups.

22
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:22.367
It caches them under a key made of latitude, longitude, and radius, which is a tuple.

23
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:26.383
A repeat question hits the cache instead of the API.

24
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:30.100
Reranker pairs of chunk id and score are tuples too.

25
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.167
One trap: immutable does not mean deep-frozen.

26
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:38.267
The tuple's slots are sealed, but a list inside it can still change.

27
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:40.295
Let's recap.

28
00:01:40.295 --> 00:01:42.433
Tuples are immutable sequences.

29
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:46.033
Immutable means hashable, so they can be dictionary keys.

30
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:49.167
Multiple return values are really one tuple.

31
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:50.966
And the comma makes it.

32
00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:52.400
Next up: dictionaries.
